00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:31.600
Collecting microbes is a dirty business.&nbsp;
When you venture out into ponds and lakes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.400
to gather samples, you don’t just bring&nbsp;
back water and microbes. You bring back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.800
bits of plants and other debris.
And that can make it difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.720
to find the organism we want to&nbsp;
talk about today: the pelomyxa.&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:07.600
Pelomyxa are giant amoeba, capable of growing as&nbsp;
much as 5 millimeters in length. So it doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:13.200
seem like they should be that difficult to find.&nbsp;
In fact, James—our master of microscopes—found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.280
hundreds of them filling up his pond tank,&nbsp;
completely visible without the help of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:26.400
microscope. They were so large that he actually&nbsp;
took one and he touched it. So, if you would like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:32.960
to know what it feels like to pet an amoeba,&nbsp;
he is now an expert. He says, “It’s squishy.”&nbsp;

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:40.080
But while they may be in our pond tank, they’re&nbsp;
often harder to pick out with a microscope.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:47.440
They move incredibly slowly, and their cytoplasm&nbsp;
tends to fill up with grains of sand and other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:52.640
particles, so they often blend into the background&nbsp;
with the other debris we find in our slide.&nbsp;

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.880
So despite being giants, the pelomyxa can&nbsp;
be deceptively difficult to recognize.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:07.840
And as we will see, that is not&nbsp;
the only tricky thing about them.&nbsp;

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:16.080
Pelomyxa was first described by the German&nbsp;
scientist Richard Greeff, who named it Pelobius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:22.480
in 1870, and then four years later decided&nbsp;
to change the name to Pelomyxa palustris.&nbsp;

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:29.520
The species is marked with a number of nonmotile&nbsp;
flagella and several nuclei, though “several”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:34.640
covers a wide range of nuclei numbers. Some&nbsp;
pelomyxa have been observed with two nuclei,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:40.720
while others have had several thousand.
The species is anaerobic, found in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:47.360
bottom sediments of freshwater where oxygen is&nbsp;
low. And like many anaerobic protists, pelomyxa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:53.280
turn themselves into a home for prokaryotes in&nbsp;
exchange for help surviving their environment.&nbsp;

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:58.320
While these endosymbionts had been observed&nbsp;
by Greeff and many others after him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:02.640
they weren’t identified until more recently,&nbsp;
thanks to the development of molecular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:10.080
techniques to extract and identify their DNA.
In describing the endosymbionts they observed how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:16.960
they help the pelomyxa survive and make nutrients,&nbsp;
the scientists compared the group to a sewage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:22.640
treatment plant. That might sound like an odd way&nbsp;
to describe eating until you realize that Pelomyxa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:28.960
eat by just engulfing stuff around them. And&nbsp;
when you’re a giant amoeba living at the bottom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:35.760
of a pond, the stuff around you is sludge.
Lots of pond sludge, though if you want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.800
to get technical about it, that sludge is&nbsp;
called sapropel. And while sludge may not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:45.680
sound particularly delicious, sapropel&nbsp;
is made up of decaying organic matter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:50.880
It is rich in nutrients, making it a&nbsp;
feast for amoeba and endosymbionts alike.&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.960
So while calling them a sewage treatment plant&nbsp;
may not seem the most glamorous comparison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:00.880
it is a life that probably works&nbsp;
well for all microbes involved.&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:12.800
In the early days of Pelomyxa discovery,&nbsp;
scientists identified more than 20 species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:18.320
of the giant amoeba. But distinguishing&nbsp;
giant amorphous species from one another is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:24.000
subtle challenge, and it became apparent in the&nbsp;
1990s that many of these species were actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:28.400
just Pelomyxa palustris in some&nbsp;
phase of its life cycle that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.600
distinct enough to look like a&nbsp;
different organism altogether.&nbsp;

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:37.920
So for a time, it was thought that maybe&nbsp;
Pelomyxa palustris was the only Pelomyxa species,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:43.280
and that its life was just so complex that&nbsp;
it encompassed all of these different forms.&nbsp;

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:49.600
But since then, scientists have managed to&nbsp;
identify a handful of other Pelomyxa species,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:53.760
making out those subtle details that&nbsp;
differentiate them from one another.&nbsp;

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:58.480
But while we’ve been describing&nbsp;
the traits the pelomyxa do have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.920
whether that’s their giant&nbsp;
nuclei or their endosymbionts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:08.240
there is one very distinctive trait&nbsp;
that they all lack: a mitochondrion.&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:14.240
This lack of a mitochondrion has been a&nbsp;
fascinating question to those studying the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:20.480
evolution of eukaryotes. Endosymbiotic theory&nbsp;
tells us that the mitochondria were once their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:28.720
own organism, until in a massive act of evolution,&nbsp;
they were consumed by another organism and stuck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:35.520
around, eventually morphing into the organelle&nbsp;
that we know and love and depend on today.&nbsp;

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:43.200
But eukaryotes like Pelomyxa that do not have a&nbsp;
mitochondrion don’t fit neatly into that story. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:50.880
in 1983, the scientist Thomas Cavalier-Smith&nbsp;
proposed a new kingdom of life: the Archezoa.&nbsp;

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:57.440
The kingdom Archezoa were composed of eukaryotes&nbsp;
who were thought to have diverged before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:03.200
evolution of mitochondrial eukaryotes. And&nbsp;
even of this strange group, the Pelomyxa and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:08.160
their fellow mitochondrion-less amoeba were&nbsp;
thought to be the most primitive because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:13.280
well, look at them. They’re amoeba. They’re&nbsp;
simple. And it’s hard not to buy into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:18.160
assumption that just because something is&nbsp;
more complex, it must be more advanced.&nbsp;

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:24.480
But the Archezoan kingdom took a big hit from&nbsp;
these supposedly primitive amoeba when it became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:31.440
apparent that they actually did have some of the&nbsp;
molecular remnants of a mitochondrion. Which meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:37.440
that even if they didn’t have a mitochondrion&nbsp;
now, they evolved from something that did.&nbsp;

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:45.440
That makes Pelomyxa something more&nbsp;
than just simple: they are degenerate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:50.160
That’s not an insult, this is what they&nbsp;
are called. They are degenerate protists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:55.520
all because at some point in their lineage,&nbsp;
a mitochondrion was gained and then lost.&nbsp;

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:02.480
And since then, the Archezoa kingdom has&nbsp;
been abandoned, undone by the evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:07.120
in its own members’ molecules. And the&nbsp;
undoing of this kingdom was, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:12.800
more than just the result of any one organism.&nbsp;
It took many scientists studying many organisms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:17.920
to come to this conclusion, and it will take many&nbsp;
more to answer the questions that still remain.&nbsp;

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:26.560
But it’s lovely to think of the Pelomyxa—our&nbsp;
very own mysterious, hidden giant—helping to undo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:31.760
some of the myths that we hold about&nbsp;
simplicity and evolution. It’s not the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:43.840
sort of story that gets passed down through&nbsp;
generations, but you know, maybe it should be.&nbsp;

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:53.680
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.&nbsp;

